name: bytes
boss: Void
flavor: Nothing you deleted is as gone as you think.
brief: Text tools stop at the first byte that is not text. `hexdump` and `od` show
brief: you what is really there. `dd` moves raw blocks with no interpretation at
brief: all, which is why it can copy a whole disk -- and why a typo in its
brief: arguments is the most expensive typo on this system.
brief:
brief: `dd` names its arguments with `=` rather than flags: `if=` in, `of=` out, `bs=`
brief: block size, `count=` how many blocks.
brief:
brief: Sizing and slicing: `truncate` sets a file's length to whatever you say,
brief: `fallocate` reserves the blocks up front so the space is already yours,
brief: `split` cuts one large file into numbered pieces you can move separately.
brief: `hexedit` changes bytes in place.
brief:
brief: `shred` overwrites a file before unlinking it. That is honest on a spinning
brief: disk and a half-truth on flash, where the controller decides which cells
brief: a write actually lands in and the old ones may still hold your data.
---
q: %show% @bin@ as hex with `hexdump`, printable text beside it
a: hexdump -C @bin@
cmp: ref
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -C
hint: C is the canonical format, and the only one worth memorising
---
q: {@bin@'s header -- `32` bytes, hex and text|dump @bin@'s `32` bytes as hex and text|cap @bin@ at `32` bytes, hex and text}, with `hexdump`
a: hexdump -C -n 32 @bin@
cmp: ref
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -n
hint: -n caps how many bytes hexdump reads, from the start
---
q: {dump @bin@ from byte 8 on, hex and text|skip @bin@'s first 8 bytes, dump hex and text|show @bin@ from byte 8 onward as hex and text}, with `hexdump`
a: hexdump -C -s 8 @bin@
cmp: ref
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -s
hint: -s skips that many bytes before the dump begins
---
setup: you're piping this into something that counts lines, and folded repeats would throw the count off
q: {@bin@ hex and text, repeats not folded to *|show every line of @bin@, repeats included|@bin@ hex and text, no asterisk for repeats}, with `hexdump`
a: hexdump -C -v @bin@
cmp: ref
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -v
hint: hexdump normally folds repeated lines into a *; -v turns that off
---
setup: another script needs to parse this dump and the usual hex-plus-text layout doesn't line up cleanly for it
q: {dump @bin@ with your own format string|dump @bin@ with a format string you supply|write your own format, dump @bin@ with it}, with `hexdump`
a: hexdump -e '16/1 "%02x " "\n"' @bin@
out: [0-9a-f]
cmp: ref
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -e
hint: -e takes a format string, the same kind -f reads from a file
---
q: {dump @txt@ in octal with `od`, the older tool that does the same job|use `od`, hexdump's older cousin, to dump @txt@ in octal|show @txt@ in octal using `od` rather than hexdump}
a: od @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: od
teach: od
hint: od's default is octal words -- no flags needed here
---
setup: another tool already gave you the offset in decimal -- octal here would only confuse the match
q: %show% @bin@'s address column with `od`, decimal not octal
a: od -A d @bin@
out: ^0000016
ref: od
teach: od -A
hint: -A picks the radix for the address column -- d decimal, x hex, n none at all
---
setup: you need to check bytes one at a time, not grouped into the default word-sized chunks
q: @bin@ is an elf binary -- %show% single hex bytes with `od`
a: od -t x1 @bin@
out: 7f 45 4c 46
ref: od
teach: od -t
hint: -t picks the display type -- x1 is hex, one byte at a time
---
q: @bin@'s signature -- %show% its first 8 bytes with `od`
a: od -N 8 @bin@
out: ^0000010$
ref: od
teach: od -N
hint: -N caps how many bytes od reads, from the start
---
q: {copy @txt@ to copy.txt using `dd`|use `dd` to copy @txt@ into copy.txt|make a copy of @txt@ named copy.txt with `dd`}
a: dd if=@txt@ of=copy.txt
state: exists:copy.txt
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: if= is the source, of= is the destination -- no other flags needed
---
q: {only need @log@'s start -- copy 512 bytes to head.bin|grab the first 512 bytes of @log@ into head.bin|pull the first 512 bytes off @log@ into head.bin}, with `dd`
a: dd if=@log@ of=head.bin bs=512 count=1
state: exists:head.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: bs= sets the block size, count= how many blocks to copy
---
setup: a binary format's spec puts a field 32 bytes in, 16 bytes long, and you need just that slice
q: {embedded 32 bytes into @log@ -- read 16 there|starting 32 bytes into @log@, read 16 more|skip 32 bytes into @log@, read 16 from there}, with `dd`
a: dd if=@log@ bs=1 skip=32 count=16
out: 16 bytes
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: skip= moves the read start point; bs=1 makes skip/count mean bytes
---
q: %make% zero.bin with `dd`, a 1 kilobyte file of zero bytes
a: dd if=/dev/zero of=zero.bin bs=1k count=1
state: exists:zero.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: /dev/zero is an endless stream of zero bytes; bs=/count= cap it
---
q: %make% rand.bin with `dd`, 32 random bytes for a key
a: dd if=/dev/urandom of=rand.bin bs=32 count=1
state: exists:rand.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: /dev/urandom is the random byte source; bs= sets how many bytes total
---
q: {copy @txt@ to copy.txt with `dd` for a script -- no progress, no summary|copy @txt@ to copy.txt with `dd`, no progress or summary output|copy @txt@ to copy.txt with `dd` as quietly as possible}
a: dd if=@txt@ of=copy.txt status=none
state: exists:copy.txt
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: status= controls dd's progress/summary output -- none silences it
---
q: {@log@ is too long to page through -- `split` it into 2-line pieces|break @log@ into 2-line pieces with `split`|cut @log@ into 2-line chunks with `split`}
a: split -l 2 @log@
state: exists:xab
ref: split
teach: split -l
hint: -l counts lines per output piece, split's default unit
---
q: {you need to move @log@ in chunks under `100` bytes -- `split` it up|break @log@ into 100-byte pieces with `split`|cut @log@ into 100-byte chunks with `split`}
a: split -b 100 @log@
state: exists:xaa
ref: split
teach: split -b
hint: -b counts bytes per piece instead of lines
---
q: {@log@ `split` into so many pieces -- widen suffixes to 3|`split` @log@ with 3-character suffixes, not the default 2|widen @log@'s `split` suffixes from 2 to 3 characters}
a: split -a 3 -l 2 @log@
state: exists:xaaa
ref: split
teach: split -a
hint: -a sets how many characters long the generated suffixes are
---
q: {open @bin@ in a hex editor to change bytes, not read them|open @bin@ in something that edits bytes, not just views them|unlike hexdump, open @bin@ in a tool that writes bytes back}
a: hexedit @bin@
why: interactive
ref: hexedit
teach: hexedit
hint: hexdump shows you bytes; this is how you change them
---
q: securely erase @txt@ with `shred`, then remove it
a: shred -u @txt@
state: gone:@txt@
ref: shred
teach: shred -u
hint: shred overwrites the bytes first; -u then unlinks the file
---
setup: someone insists three passes isn't enough and wants the old-school seven
q: overwrite @txt@ seven times with `shred` before removing it
a: shred -n 7 -u @txt@
state: gone:@txt@
ref: shred
teach: shred -n
hint: -n sets how many overwrite passes; the default is three
---
setup: you don't want it obvious afterward that this file was ever wiped
q: `shred` @txt@ with a final zero pass, then remove it
a: shred -z -u @txt@
state: gone:@txt@
ref: shred
teach: shred -z
hint: -z adds a last zero pass
---
setup: you need a megabyte-sized file to test with and no megabyte to spend on it
q: %make% a 1-megabyte file `sparse.img` with `truncate`
a: truncate -s 1M sparse.img
state: exists:sparse.img
ref: truncate
teach: truncate -s
hint: -s sets the size; the file reads as that many bytes without using the space
---
setup: you need the space guaranteed now, not created as a hole that might fail to fill later
q: preallocate 64 kilobytes of real space for `blob.bin` with `fallocate`
a: fallocate -l 64k blob.bin
state: exists:blob.bin
ref: fallocate
teach: fallocate -l
hint: -l is the length to reserve; unlike truncate this claims the blocks now
